// ECON question bank. Written from the LMS Module 1-5 Ebooks and Study Guides only.
// Row: [module, question, options, correctIndex (0-based), reason, source, wrong-option notes]
window.QBANK = {
  subject: "Managerial Economics", code: "ECON",
  modules: {"1":"Nature & Scope of Managerial Economics","2":"Demand Analysis","3":"Cost Concept & Production Theory","4":"Pricing Decisions","5":"Profit & Inflation"},
  rows: [
[1,"According to which scholar is Managerial Economics defined as 'applying economic concepts to rational decision-making'?",["Spencer","Mansfield","Douglas","Haynes"],1,"Mansfield defined ME as the application of economic concepts and methods to rational business decision-making.","Ebook","Other options: Spencer defined ME as integration of economic theory with business practice — not rational decision-making. · Douglas defined ME as rules and principles to attain economic goals of management. · Haynes defined ME as economic tools relevant to the firm's decision-making process."],
[1,"Spencer's definition of Managerial Economics emphasizes which core idea?",["Rules to attain economic goals","Integration of economic theory with business practice","Economic tools for decision-making","Applying concepts to rational choices"],1,"Spencer defined ME as the integration of economic theory with business practice.","Ebook","Other options: Rules to attain economic goals = Douglas. · Economic tools for decision-making = Haynes. · Rational choice application = Mansfield."],
[1,"Managerial Economics is described as a 'Science' because it involves:",["Applied judgment in practice","Systematic body of knowledge with tested policies","Helping management take rational decisions","Changes with human behaviour"],1,"ME as a science means it is a systematic body of knowledge with tested policies that are universally applicable.","Ebook","Other options: Applied judgment = Art dimension. · Helping take rational decisions = Administrative tool. · Changes with human behaviour = Dynamic nature."],
[1,"Which type of Managerial Economics follows market and customer demand in a democratic, market-driven manner?",["Normative","Radical","Liberal","Positive"],2,"Liberal ME follows market forces and customer demand — democratic and market-driven approach.","Ebook","Other options: Normative ME is based on real-world experience such as demand forecasting and cost management. · Radical ME focuses on changing the status quo and prioritizes customer satisfaction over profit. · Positive economics describes what is, not what should be — not a ME type in this classification."],
[1,"Which type of Managerial Economics focuses on changing the status quo and customer satisfaction over profit maximization?",["Liberal","Normative","Radical","Applied"],2,"Radical ME challenges existing norms, prioritizing customer satisfaction and social change over profit.","Ebook","Other options: Liberal = market-driven and democratic. · Normative = practical, based on real-world experience. · Applied is not one of the three ME types."],
[1,"Mankiw's 10th principle states that prices rise when the government:",["Increases taxes","Reduces interest rates","Prints too much money","Imposes trade tariffs"],2,"Mankiw's 10th principle: prices rise when the government prints too much money — a core inflation insight.","Ebook","Other options: Taxes affect fiscal policy but are not Mankiw's 10th principle. · Interest rate reduction is monetary policy — linked to Principle 9 (economy-wide trade-offs). · Trade tariffs relate to international economics, not this principle."],
[1,"Mankiw's principle that 'rational people think at the margin' is called:",["Opportunity Cost","Tradeoff","Marginalism","Incentive principle"],2,"Mankiw's Principle 3 — Marginalism: rational people compare incremental gains against incremental costs.","Ebook","Other options: Opportunity cost = Principle 2 — cost of the next best alternative foregone. · Tradeoff = Principle 1 — every choice involves sacrificing something. · Incentives = Principle 4 — people respond to positive and negative incentives."],
[1,"Which firm objective model uses the formula U = f(S, M, D)?",["Baumol's Sales Maximisation","Cyert and March's Satisficing","Williamson's Utility Maximisation","Profit Maximisation"],2,"Williamson's Managerial Utility model: U = f(S, M, D) — Staff (S), Managerial emoluments (M), Discretionary investment (D).","Ebook","Other options: Baumol's model = Sales Maximisation — maximise revenue subject to minimum profit. · Cyert & March = Satisficing — aspiration-level targets, not a utility function formula. · Profit Maximisation = MR = MC — not a utility function."],
[1,"The Sales Maximisation objective of the firm is associated with which economist?",["Williamson","Cyert and March","Baumol","Marris"],2,"Baumol's Sales Maximisation model holds that firms aim to maximise total sales revenue subject to a minimum profit constraint.","Ebook","Other options: Williamson = Managerial Utility Maximisation (U = f(S,M,D)). · Cyert and March = Behavioural Satisficing model. · Marris = Managerial Growth model (not the same as sales max)."],
[1,"The Satisficing model — where firms aim to meet aspiration-level targets — was proposed by:",["Baumol","Williamson","Cyert and March","Simon"],2,"Cyert and March's behavioural theory introduced the satisficing concept — firms set aspirational targets and are satisfied when met.","Ebook","Other options: Baumol = Sales Maximisation. · Williamson = Managerial Utility Maximisation. · Simon coined the term 'satisficing' but Cyert and March developed the firm-level model in the ebook."],
[1,"Value Maximisation as a firm objective uses the formula:",["U = f(S,M,D)","MR = MC","V = A / (1 + i)^n","D = πR - π0 - T"],2,"Value Maximisation: V = A / (1+i)^n — present value of future cash flows, where A = cash flow, i = discount rate, n = time.","Ebook","Other options: U = f(S,M,D) = Williamson's Utility model. · MR = MC = Profit Maximisation condition. · D = πR - π0 - T = Williamson's Discretionary Investment formula."],
[1,"The Equimarginal Principle in Managerial Economics states that resources should be allocated until:",["Total output is maximised","Marginal product of each input equals one","VMPs of all inputs divided by their prices are equal","Fixed costs equal variable costs"],2,"Equimarginal: VMPL/PL = VMPK/PK = ... — value of marginal product per rupee spent must be equal across all inputs.","Ebook","Other options: Total output max is a result, not the condition. · Marginal product equalling one is not the criterion. · Fixed = variable cost is the break-even concept — unrelated."],
[1,"Opportunity cost in Managerial Economics refers to:",["The total cost of production","The income foregone from the next best alternative","The sunk cost already incurred","The variable cost per unit"],1,"Opportunity cost = income or benefit sacrificed by not choosing the next best alternative use of resources.","Ebook","Other options: Total cost of production = accounting cost concept. · Sunk cost = already incurred costs that cannot be recovered — different from opportunity cost. · Variable cost = costs that change with output — a production cost concept."],
[1,"Which economic concept involves evaluating decisions based on marginal or incremental changes rather than total changes?",["Discounting principle","Time perspective","Equimarginal principle","Incremental concept"],3,"The Incremental Concept focuses on marginal/incremental changes to guide decisions — change in total cost or revenue from one extra unit.","Ebook","Other options: Discounting = present value calculation (future costs/benefits adjusted for time value). · Time perspective = distinguishing short-run vs long-run implications. · Equimarginal = optimal allocation across inputs — different from incremental analysis."],
[1,"Managerial Economics at the micro level deals with which of the following?",["Government policy and exchange rates","Employment levels and business cycles","Demand, price, output, inputs, and substitutes at firm level","National income and price levels"],2,"Micro component of ME = firm-level analysis: demand, price, output, input decisions, and substitutes.","Ebook","Other options: Government policy, exchange rates = macro external factors. · Employment and business cycles = macro component of ME. · National income = macroeconomics, not micro ME."],
[1,"Mankiw's Principle 7 states that governments can sometimes improve market outcomes. This is based on which concept?",["Externalities and market failure","Invisible hand","Trade leads to gains","People respond to incentives"],0,"Principle 7: Governments can improve outcomes when markets fail due to externalities or market power — market failure justification.","Ebook","Other options: Invisible hand = Principle 6 — markets usually allocate resources efficiently. · Trade gains = Principles 5 — trade can make everyone better off. · Incentives = Principle 4 — people respond to incentives."],
[1,"Which of Mankiw's principles addresses the economy-wide trade-off between inflation and unemployment?",["Principle 6","Principle 8","Principle 9","Principle 10"],2,"Principle 9: In the short run, society faces a trade-off between inflation and unemployment (Phillips curve).","Ebook","Other options: Principle 6 = markets are usually a good way to organize economic activity. · Principle 8 = a country's standard of living depends on its ability to produce goods. · Principle 10 = prices rise when government prints too much money."],
[1,"The Discounting Principle in Managerial Economics is used to:",["Calculate marginal revenue","Evaluate future costs and benefits in present value terms","Set the equimarginal allocation of inputs","Determine the break-even point"],1,"Discounting converts future cash flows to present values using V = A/(1+i)^n, enabling comparison of costs/benefits across time.","Ebook","Other options: Marginal revenue = revenue concept, not discounting. · Equimarginal allocation = separate principle about input ratios. · Break-even = cost-volume-profit analysis — Module 5 topic."],
[1,"Douglas's definition of Managerial Economics focuses on:",["Applying economic tools to decision-making processes","Rules and principles to attain economic goals of management","Integration of economic theory with business practice","Applying concepts to rational decision-making"],1,"Douglas: ME provides rules and principles that help management attain economic objectives of the firm.","Ebook","Other options: Economic tools for decision-making = Haynes. · Integration of theory with practice = Spencer. · Rational decision-making = Mansfield."],
[1,"The Profit Maximisation model holds that equilibrium is achieved when:",["TR = TC","MC = MR","AR = MR","AFC = AVC"],1,"Profit is maximised when MC = MR (and MC cuts MR from below). This is the neoclassical equilibrium condition.","Ebook","Other options: TR = TC is the break-even point, not profit max. · AR = MR occurs under perfect competition — not the general profit max rule. · AFC = AVC has no direct significance as a profit max condition."],
[1,"Managerial Economics is primarily a bridge between which two disciplines?",["Accounting and Finance","Economics and Management","Statistics and Marketing","Law and Operations"],1,"ME bridges Economics (resource allocation theory) and Management (decision-making in practice) — its core defining characteristic.","Ebook","Other options: Accounting and Finance is related but too narrow. · Statistics is a tool, not the discipline ME bridges. · Law and Operations are not the primary bridge of ME."],
[1,"Mankiw's Principle 1 — 'People face tradeoffs' — is best illustrated by which concept?",["Diminishing returns","Opportunity cost","Guns vs butter dilemma","Invisible hand"],2,"The classic guns vs butter dilemma (defence vs consumer goods) illustrates the tradeoff — producing more of one requires less of the other.","Ebook","Other options: Diminishing returns = production principle, not Principle 1. · Opportunity cost = Principle 2 (cost of choice), not the tradeoff itself. · Invisible hand = Principle 6 — market efficiency."],
[1,"Which dimension of Managerial Economics describes it as 'an administrative tool that helps management make rational decisions'?",["Science","Art","Administrative tool","Dynamic nature"],2,"Administrative tool dimension: ME aids executives in making systematic, rational choices within the firm.","Ebook","Other options: Science = systematic body of tested knowledge. · Art = applied judgment; theory into practice. · Dynamic = changes with human behaviour and environment."],
[1,"Time Perspective in Managerial Economics involves:",["Calculating present value of future returns","Distinguishing between short-run and long-run cost implications","Allocating resources at the margin","Measuring opportunity cost over time"],1,"Time perspective: managers must distinguish SR (some fixed costs) from LR (all costs variable) implications of their decisions.","Ebook","Other options: Present value = Discounting principle. · Marginal allocation = Equimarginal principle. · Opportunity cost is a separate concept — not the time perspective."],
[2,"Demand is defined as the consumer's willingness AND which other factor to purchase a good at a given price?",["Preference","Ability","Access","Awareness"],1,"Demand = willingness + ability to purchase at a given price. Ability (purchasing power) is as critical as willingness.","Ebook","Other options: Preference alone does not constitute demand without ability to pay. · Access relates to distribution/supply, not demand definition. · Awareness affects marketing, not the economic definition of demand."],
[2,"Which is NOT one of the 6 objectives of Demand Analysis?",["Forecasting sales and revenues","Guidance for manipulation of demand","Setting employee compensation levels","Appraising salesman's performance"],2,"The 6 objectives are forecasting, demand manipulation, market influence analysis, salesman appraisal, competitive strength, and inventory/working capital planning. Employee compensation is HR, not demand analysis.","Ebook","Other options: Forecasting sales = objective 1. · Guidance for demand manipulation = objective 2. · Appraising salesman's performance = objective 4."],
[2,"The Law of Demand states that price and quantity demanded have a/an:",["Direct (positive) relationship","Inverse (negative) relationship","No relationship","Proportional relationship"],1,"Law of Demand: as price rises, quantity demanded falls (and vice versa) — inverse relationship, shown by downward sloping demand curve.","Ebook","Other options: Direct/positive relationship is the Law of Supply (not demand). · No relationship is incorrect — demand is price-sensitive. · Proportional relationship applies to Unitary Elasticity (Ed=1), not the general law."],
[2,"When the price of coffee rises, demand for tea increases. This makes coffee and tea:",["Complements (Exy negative)","Inferior goods","Substitutes (Exy positive)","Normal goods"],2,"Substitutes have positive cross elasticity: rise in price of one (coffee) increases demand for the other (tea). Exy > 0.","Ebook","Other options: Complements have negative Exy — rise in price of one decreases demand for the other. · Inferior goods are defined by income elasticity, not cross elasticity. · Normal goods are defined by Ey > 0, not cross price relationship."],
[2,"Price Elasticity of Demand (Ed) is calculated as:",["% change in Price / % change in Quantity","% change in Quantity / % change in Price","% change in Income / % change in Quantity","Change in TR / Change in Price"],1,"Ed = % ΔQ / % ΔP — the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price.","Ebook","Other options: % ΔP / % ΔQ is the inverse — slope-related but not Ed. · % ΔIncome / % ΔQ would be the inverse of income elasticity. · Change in TR / Change in price is MR, not Ed."],
[2,"If price changes by 10% and quantity demanded changes by 20%, the demand is:",["Inelastic (Ed = 0.5)","Unitary (Ed = 1)","Elastic (Ed = 2)","Perfectly elastic (Ed = infinity)"],2,"Ed = 20%/10% = 2 > 1, so demand is elastic. The quantity response is proportionally larger than the price change.","Ebook","Other options: Inelastic = Ed < 1 — quantity changes less proportionately than price. · Unitary = Ed = 1 — TR is maximised at this point. · Perfectly elastic = Ed = infinity — horizontal demand curve, infinite response to any price change."],
[2,"A perfectly inelastic demand curve is:",["Horizontal (Ed = infinity)","Downward sloping (Ed = 1)","Vertical (Ed = 0)","Upward sloping"],2,"Perfectly inelastic (Ed = 0): demand is unresponsive to any price change — represented by a vertical demand curve.","Ebook","Other options: Horizontal demand curve = perfectly elastic (Ed = infinity). · Downward sloping = normal demand curve (Ed between 0 and infinity). · Upward sloping demand = Giffen goods — an exception, not Ed = 0."],
[2,"When Ey (Income Elasticity of Demand) is negative, the good is classified as:",["Luxury good","Normal good","Necessity","Inferior good"],3,"Ey < 0: as income rises, demand falls — characteristic of inferior goods (e.g., coarse grain, margarine).","Ebook","Other options: Luxury = Ey > 1 (demand rises more than proportionately with income). · Normal = Ey > 0 (demand rises with income). · Necessity = 0 < Ey < 1 (demand rises less than proportionately)."],
[2,"If income falls by 20% (from $50k to $40k) and car demand falls by 50% (from 10k to 5k), the income elasticity is:",["0.5 — necessity","1.0 — unitary","2.5 — luxury","-2.5 — inferior"],2,"Ey = (-50%) / (-20%) = 2.5 — highly positive, meaning cars are a luxury good highly sensitive to income changes.","Ebook","Other options: 0.5 would indicate a necessity (Ey between 0 and 1). · Unitary (Ey=1) means demand changes proportionately with income. · -2.5 would indicate an inferior good — income and demand move in opposite directions."],
[2,"The Substitution Effect occurs when a price rise causes consumers to:",["Have less real income to spend","Switch to cheaper alternative goods","Demand more of the good","Save more money"],1,"Substitution effect: when a good's price rises, it becomes relatively expensive — consumers switch to cheaper alternatives.","Ebook","Other options: Less real income = Income effect (not substitution). · Demand more of the good would be an upward-sloping demand — not substitution effect. · Save more money is an income effect behaviour — unrelated to substitution."],
[2,"At unitary price elasticity (Ed = 1), which of the following is true about Total Revenue?",["TR is minimised","TR falls when price falls","TR is maximised, and MR = 0","TR increases when price rises"],2,"At Ed = 1, TR is at its maximum. At this point, MR = 0 — any further price change reduces total revenue.","Ebook","Other options: TR is minimised — incorrect; it is maximised at Ed = 1. · When Ed > 1 (elastic), TR falls when price rises and rises when price falls. · When Ed < 1 (inelastic), TR increases when price rises."],
[2,"Average Revenue (AR) is always equal to:",["Marginal Revenue (MR)","Total Revenue (TR)","Price (P)","Variable Cost"],2,"AR = TR/Q = (P × Q)/Q = P. Average Revenue always equals price — proven algebraically.","Ebook","Other options: AR = MR only under perfect competition (horizontal demand curve). · AR = TR would be wrong — TR is total, AR is per unit. · Variable cost is a cost concept — unrelated to revenue."],
[2,"If 10 chairs sell for Rs.160 each (TR = Rs.1600) and 11 chairs sell for TR = Rs.1780, the MR of the 11th chair is:",["Rs.160","Rs.162","Rs.180","Rs.178"],2,"MR of 11th chair = TR11 - TR10 = 1780 - 1600 = Rs.180.","Ebook","Other options: Rs.160 = average revenue/price per chair, not marginal revenue. · Rs.162 would be incorrect calculation. · Rs.178 has no basis in the given data."],
[2,"Total Revenue (TR) is equal to the sum of all:",["Average Revenues","Fixed Costs","Marginal Revenues","Variable Costs"],2,"TR = ΣMR — Total Revenue is the summation of all Marginal Revenues for each unit produced.","Ebook","Other options: TR ≠ sum of AR (AR = price per unit; TR = price × total quantity). · Fixed costs are cost-side, unrelated to revenue summation. · Variable costs are cost concepts — TR is purely revenue."],
[2,"The Backward-Bending Labour Supply Curve results from which two effects acting in opposite directions?",["Price effect and income effect","Substitution effect and income effect","Demand effect and supply effect","Wealth effect and substitution effect"],1,"As wages rise: substitution effect dominates first (work more is worthwhile), then income effect dominates (can maintain income with fewer hours) — creating backward bend.","Ebook","Other options: Price effect is not a standard term here — substitution effect is the correct term. · Demand and supply effect are aggregate market concepts, not the labour supply explanation. · Wealth effect is related but not the standard textbook framework here."],
[2,"Which demand forecasting method is based on past data and the formula O = T × S × C × I?",["Survey of Buyer's Intentions","Sales Force Composite","Trend Projection","Expert's Opinion"],2,"Trend Projection uses historical data. O = T × S × C × I (Trend × Seasonal × Cyclical × Irregular).","Ebook","Other options: Survey of Buyer's Intentions = direct consumer survey — short-term, expensive. · Sales Force Composite = salesperson estimates — subjective, not past data formula. · Expert's Opinion = external specialist views — no past data formula involved."],
[2,"Which demand forecasting method is best for new products with no historical data?",["Trend Projection","Controlled Experiments","Expert's Opinion","Economic Indicators"],2,"Expert's Opinion uses external specialist views — suitable for new products where no past data exists.","Ebook","Other options: Trend Projection requires past data — not applicable for new products. · Controlled Experiments are expensive and risky but do measure price elasticity. · Economic Indicators link demand to leading indicators — needs an established pattern."],
[2,"Cross elasticity between complements (e.g., printer and ink) is:",["Positive","Zero","Negative","Greater than 1"],2,"Complements have negative cross elasticity: if printer price rises, demand for ink (complement) falls — Exy < 0.","Ebook","Other options: Positive Exy = substitutes (demand for one rises when price of other rises). · Zero Exy = unrelated goods (no cross-price relationship). · Greater than 1 Exy = strong substitutes, not complements."],
[2,"Price discrimination between elastic and inelastic markets is an application of:",["Income elasticity","Cross elasticity","Price elasticity of demand","Demand forecasting"],2,"Price discrimination uses Ed: charge higher price in inelastic segment, lower price in elastic segment to maximise revenue.","Ebook","Other options: Income elasticity determines product category (luxury/necessity), not price discrimination strategy. · Cross elasticity measures substitute/complement relationships — different application. · Demand forecasting predicts future demand levels — not directly about discrimination."],
[2,"When a firm charges different prices for different uses of the same commodity (e.g., domestic vs commercial electricity pricing), this is:",["Personal discrimination","Geographical discrimination","Discrimination according to use","Second-degree discrimination"],2,"Discrimination by use: same product/service charged differently based on how it is used (domestic vs commercial electricity).","Ebook","Other options: Personal = different prices per individual (doctor example). · Geographical = different prices in different markets/regions. · Second-degree = quantity discounts — different price per quantity consumed."],
[2,"The AR curve of a firm is equivalent to its:",["Marginal Cost curve","Demand curve","Supply curve","Total Revenue curve"],1,"Since AR = Price, the AR curve traces price-quantity combinations — it is identical to the firm's demand curve.","Ebook","Other options: Marginal cost curve is a separate cost-side concept. · Supply curve represents what the firm is willing to produce at each price — different from demand/AR. · Total Revenue curve = TR, not AR — TR is price × quantity, not per-unit revenue."],
[2,"When demand is inelastic (Ed < 1) and the price is increased, Total Revenue will:",["Fall","Stay the same","Rise","First rise then fall"],2,"Inelastic demand: % fall in quantity < % rise in price. TR = P × Q rises because the price increase outweighs the quantity fall.","Ebook","Other options: TR falls when demand is elastic (Ed > 1) and price rises. · TR stays same only at Ed = 1 (unitary) — not inelastic. · First rise then fall pattern does not apply to standard inelastic demand."],
[2,"Which demand forecasting method involves varying price and advertising in test markets to measure price elasticity?",["Survey of Buyer's Intentions","Controlled Experiments","Sales Force Composite","Economic Indicators"],1,"Controlled Experiments: deliberately vary price/promotion in test markets to measure consumer response and elasticity. Expensive and risky.","Ebook","Other options: Survey of Buyer's Intentions = asking consumers directly about future purchases. · Sales Force Composite = salesperson estimates of future demand. · Economic Indicators = linking demand to macro-level leading indicators."],
[3,"Sunk costs are best described as:",["Costs that vary with output","Costs that cannot be recovered and are irrelevant to current decisions","Future costs estimated for planning","Costs borne by society for private production"],1,"Sunk costs: already incurred and non-recoverable (e.g., past advertising spend, R&D, machinery already purchased).","Ebook","Other options: Variable costs change with output — they are NOT sunk. · Future costs for planning = cost estimation — forward-looking. · Social costs are borne by society (pollution etc.) — separate classification."],
[3,"The Average Fixed Cost (AFC) curve takes the shape of a:",["U-shaped curve","Vertical straight line","Rectangular hyperbola","Upward sloping line"],2,"AFC = TFC / Q. As Q rises, AFC falls continuously, approaching (but never reaching) zero — forming a rectangular hyperbola.","Ebook","Other options: U-shaped = AVC and ATC curves — not AFC. · Vertical straight line = perfectly inelastic demand — not AFC. · Upward sloping = supply curve or MC after optimum — not AFC."],
[3,"The Marginal Cost (MC) curve intersects the ATC curve at:",["Its maximum point","Its minimum point","Where ATC equals AFC","Where ATC equals AVC"],1,"MC cuts ATC (and AVC) at their minimum points. When MC < AC, it pulls AC down; when MC > AC, it pulls AC up.","Ebook","Other options: Maximum point is incorrect — MC crosses at the minimum, not maximum. · ATC = AFC is not a meaningful condition (this would imply AVC = 0). · ATC = AVC would mean AFC = 0 — impossible in the short run."],
[3,"The minimum point of ATC occurs at a _____ level of output compared to the minimum point of AVC:",["Lower","Same","Higher","Equal"],2,"ATC minimum comes LATER (higher output) than AVC minimum because falling AFC continues to offset rising AVC for a while.","Ebook","Other options: Lower would be incorrect — AFC keeps pulling ATC down even after AVC starts rising. · Same level would mean AFC has no effect after AVC starts rising — wrong. · Equal is the same as 'same' — incorrect."],
[3,"In the long run, compared to the short run:",["Only variable costs exist; fixed costs disappear","Only fixed costs exist","All costs become variable","AFC curve becomes U-shaped"],2,"In the LR, there are NO fixed costs — the firm has sufficient time to adjust all inputs. ALL costs become variable.","Ebook","Other options: Variable costs don't completely eliminate fixed costs in SR — both exist in SR. · Fixed costs dominate in the very SR — in LR they disappear. · AFC becomes U-shaped is wrong — AFC falls continuously in SR and doesn't exist in LR."],
[3,"The Long-Run Average Cost (LRAC) curve is called the 'envelope curve' because it:",["Encloses all variable costs","Is tangent to (touches) each short-run ATC curve at one point","Wraps around the minimum points of all SR curves","Is always U-shaped"],1,"LRAC is drawn tangent to each SR-ATC curve. It envelops the family of SR curves, representing the least-cost option at each output level.","Ebook","Other options: It doesn't enclose variable costs — it wraps the entire family of SR-ATC curves. · It touches each SR curve at one point (not necessarily the minimum). · Empirically, LRAC is L-shaped (not U-shaped)."],
[3,"Network economies of scale are BEST exemplified by:",["Walmart's bulk buying","eBay's near-zero cost per additional user","A firm hiring specialist managers","A company getting an IPO"],1,"Network economies: online businesses where each additional customer costs nearly nothing — almost all revenue is profit (e.g., eBay).","Ebook","Other options: Walmart's bulk buying = monopsony economies of scale. · Hiring specialist managers = managerial economies of scale. · IPO access = financial economies of scale."],
[3,"Zero-Base Budgeting (ZBB) was first developed by:",["State of Georgia in 1973","Texas Instruments, USA in 1971","US Defense Department in 1960","Ford Motor Company in 1968"],1,"ZBB was originally developed by Texas Instruments of USA in 1971. It was subsequently applied by the State of Georgia in 1973.","Ebook","Other options: State of Georgia applied ZBB in 1973 — but did not develop it. · US Defense Department pioneered performance budgeting in the 1960s — not ZBB. · Ford Motor Company is not credited with ZBB in the ebook."],
[3,"Which type of variance arises from the difference between the standard price and actual price paid for materials?",["Materials Usage Variance","Materials Mix Variance","Materials Price Variance","Materials Yield Variance"],2,"Materials Price Variance = Standard Price − Actual Price paid for materials. It is one of the four material cost variance sub-types.","Ebook","Other options: Usage Variance = standard quantity specified vs actual quantity used. · Mix Variance = difference in composition/proportion of materials mix. · Yield Variance = standard yield vs actual yield obtained."],
[3,"Cost Control is a _____ function while Cost Reduction is a _____ function:",["Corrective / Preventive","Preventive / Corrective","Future / Past","Dynamic / Static"],1,"Cost Control = preventive (prevents waste within existing standards, emphasis on past/present). Cost Reduction = corrective (real permanent reduction, future-oriented).","Ebook","Other options: Corrective/Preventive is reversed — control is preventive, reduction is corrective. · Future/Past is also reversed — control focuses on past/present, reduction on future. · Dynamic/Static has no basis in this context."],
[3,"The Survivor Technique for estimating long-run costs was developed by:",["Stigler","Arrow","Cobb and Douglas","Revankar"],0,"Prof. Stigler developed the Survivor Technique — firms in the most efficient size range gain market share over time, implying their costs are lower.","Ebook","Other options: Arrow (along with Chenery, Minhas, Solow) developed the CES production function. · Cobb and Douglas = Cobb-Douglas production function (Q = AL^α K^β). · Revankar (with Bruno and Knox Lovell) worked on VES production function."],
[3,"In the Cobb-Douglas Production Function Q = AL^α K^β, when α + β > 1, this indicates:",["Constant returns to scale","Decreasing returns to scale","Increasing returns to scale","Zero returns to scale"],2,"α + β > 1 = Increasing Returns to Scale (IRS). α + β = 1 = Constant Returns. α + β < 1 = Decreasing Returns.","Ebook","Other options: Constant returns = α + β = 1 exactly. · Decreasing returns = α + β < 1. · Zero returns is not a standard category."],
[3,"The CES (Constant Elasticity of Substitution) Production Function was developed by:",["Cobb and Douglas","Arrow, Chenery, Minhas and Solow","Stigler","Walker and Clark"],1,"Arrow, Chenery, Minhas and Solow developed the CES production function as a more general form than Cobb-Douglas.","Ebook","Other options: Cobb and Douglas = Cobb-Douglas function (Q = AL^α K^β). · Stigler = Survivor Technique for LR cost estimation. · Walker and Clark are profit theorists — not production function developers."],
[3,"Minimum Efficient Scale (MES) is defined as:",["The scale at which TC is minimised","The largest output a firm can produce","The smallest output at which LAC is at its minimum","The output where AFC equals AVC"],2,"MES = smallest plant size (output level) at which Long-Run Average Cost (LAC) reaches its minimum. Firms below MES have higher costs.","Ebook","Other options: TC minimisation is break-even related — not MES. · Largest output is capacity — not MES definition. · AFC = AVC has no standard significance — not MES."],
[3,"An indifference curve shows combinations of two goods that give the consumer:",["Maximum utility","Equal satisfaction (consumer is indifferent)","Zero utility","The lowest possible cost"],1,"An indifference curve: all combinations of two goods providing equal satisfaction — the consumer is indifferent between any point on the curve.","Ebook","Other options: Maximum utility = corner solution or budget constraint tangency — not the curve itself. · Zero utility would be the axes, not the curve. · Lowest possible cost = isocost line — not an indifference curve."],
[3,"Optimal consumption according to indifference curve analysis occurs where:",["Indifference curve is at its steepest","Two indifference curves intersect","Indifference curve is tangent to the budget constraint","Marginal Revenue equals Marginal Cost"],2,"Optimal consumption = tangency point of the highest attainable indifference curve with the budget constraint line (MRS = price ratio).","Ebook","Other options: Steepest point of IC = corner solution, not general optimum. · Two ICs never intersect — intersection would contradict the definition. · MR = MC is a firm's production equilibrium — not consumer indifference analysis."],
[3,"Monopsony power as an economy of scale means a firm can:",["Produce more using the same inputs","Reduce per-unit input costs through bulk buying","Enter new markets easily","Hire better managers than competitors"],1,"Monopsony economies: buying inputs in such large quantities that per-unit input prices fall (e.g., Walmart's 'everyday low prices' from supplier bargaining).","Ebook","Other options: Producing more from same inputs = technical efficiency, not monopsony. · Entering new markets = market strategy — unrelated to monopsony economies. · Hiring better managers = managerial economies of scale."],
[3,"The empirically observed shape of the Long-Run Average Cost (LAC) curve in cross-section studies is:",["U-shaped","Horizontal straight line","L-shaped","Inverted U-shaped"],2,"Cross-section studies reveal that LAC is L-shaped: significant economies at low output, then levels off — not the theoretical U-shape.","Ebook","Other options: U-shaped = the theoretical prediction from economic theory. · Horizontal = perfectly constant returns — not observed empirically. · Inverted U-shape has no basis in cost theory."],
[3,"The basic production function is expressed as Q = f(L, C, N). What does 'N' represent?",["Labour","Capital","Land","Network"],2,"Q = f(L, C, N): L = Labour, C = Capital, N = Land. All three are the classical factors of production.","Ebook","Other options: Labour = L in the formula. · Capital = C in the formula. · Network is not a traditional factor of production in this context."],
[3,"Value Analysis as a cost reduction technique aims to:",["Increase product variety","Eliminate costs that don't contribute to functional value","Maximise overhead costs","Standardise wages across departments"],1,"Value Analysis: identify and eliminate costs that don't add to a product's function, specification, or value — without reducing quality.","Ebook","Other options: Increasing product variety = opposite of value analysis (variety reduction is a separate technique). · Maximising overhead is contrary to cost reduction. · Standardising wages = HR/labour relations — not value analysis."],
[3,"In the short-run, if AFC falls but AVC rises, which condition causes ATC to still fall?",["Rise in AVC equals rise in AFC","Drop in AFC is more than the rise in AVC","Drop in AFC is less than the rise in AVC","AFC and AVC are equal"],1,"ATC = AFC + AVC. ATC continues to fall as long as the drop in AFC exceeds the rise in AVC — net effect is still negative.","Ebook","Other options: Equal rise and fall means ATC stays constant — not falls. · Drop in AFC less than rise in AVC means ATC rises. · AFC = AVC is not a meaningful cost relationship here."],
[3,"In Budgetary Control, a Flexible Budget differs from a Fixed Budget in that it:",["Covers a longer time period","Uses zero-base as starting point","Adapts to changes in the level of activity/output","Focuses only on capital expenditures"],2,"Flexible (variable) budget adapts to output changes — provision is made for varying production levels, unlike fixed budget which is set for one output level.","Ebook","Other options: Longer time period describes long-term budgets — not the flexible/fixed distinction. · Zero-base as starting point = ZBB — a separate budgeting method. · Capital expenditures only = Capital Budget — different classification."],
[3,"Terotechnology as a cost reduction technique is defined as a combination of management and engineering practices applied to:",["Human resource development","Physical assets to achieve economic lifecycle costs","Marketing and distribution","Financial management"],1,"Terotechnology: management + engineering practices applied to physical assets across their full productive life to minimise lifecycle costs.","Ebook","Other options: Human resource development = O&M / Work Study — not terotechnology. · Marketing and distribution = selling function — not terotechnology. · Financial management = budgeting, standard costing — different domain."],
[4,"According to Needles, Anderson and Caldwell, which are the THREE major influences on pricing decisions?",["Costs, Government, Technology","Customers, Competitors, Costs","Demand, Supply, Profit","Quality, Brand, Market"],1,"The three major influences: Customers (willingness to pay), Competitors (reaction to pricing), and Costs (affect supply and profitability).","Ebook","Other options: Government is an external factor but not one of the three major influences. · Demand/Supply/Profit are economic outcomes — not the three influences. · Quality/Brand/Market are marketing factors — not the pricing framework."],
[4,"Full Cost Plus Pricing is BEST suited for which situation?",["Pricing to utilise spare capacity","Long-term contracts with government agencies","New market entry to undercut competitors","Pricing for maximum contribution to fixed costs"],1,"Full Cost Plus is economically justified for long-term contracts (govt agencies, custom products) where price = full cost + markup is mandated.","Ebook","Other options: Spare capacity utilisation = Marginal Cost Plus pricing (covers variable costs). · Undercutting competitors = Marginal or Differential Cost Plus pricing. · Maximum contribution focus = Marginal Cost Plus / contribution margin approach."],
[4,"Marginal Cost Plus Pricing uses which cost as its base?",["Full absorption cost","Standard cost","Variable (marginal) cost only","Differential cost including some fixed"],2,"Marginal Cost Plus ignores fixed costs in the base — only variable costs are used. Fixed costs must be recovered through a higher profit markup.","Ebook","Other options: Full absorption cost = Full Cost Plus method. · Standard cost = Standard Cost pricing method. · Differential cost (variable + some fixed) = Differential Cost Plus method."],
[4,"Target Cost is derived by:",["Adding profit margin to variable cost","Subtracting desired profit from target price","Adding target profit to full cost","Dividing total revenue by quantity"],1,"Target Cost = Target Price − Desired Profit Margin. This forces managers to work backwards from market price to an achievable cost.","Ebook","Other options: Adding margin to variable cost = Marginal Cost Plus pricing approach. · Adding target profit to full cost = Full Cost Plus — the reverse logic. · TR / Q = Average Revenue/Price — revenue concept, not cost."],
[4,"Pareto's 80/20 principle applied to pricing states that approximately:",["80% of products generate 100% of revenue","80% of sales revenue comes from 20% of products","20% of customers generate all revenue","80% of costs come from 80% of products"],1,"Vilfredo Pareto: 80% of a firm's sales revenue comes from just 20% of its products — used to prioritise pricing strategies.","Ebook","Other options: 80% from 100% would mean 20% generate no revenue — not the rule. · Customer focus is one application but the pricing statement focuses on products. · 80% costs from 80% products reverses the 80/20 asymmetry — not Pareto."],
[4,"The economic approach to pricing states that profits are maximised at:",["The highest possible price","Where TR = TC","Where MR = MC","Where AR = AFC"],2,"Economic theory: profit is maximised at the output where MR = MC — firms produce as long as each additional unit adds more revenue than cost.","Ebook","Other options: Highest price would maximise price per unit, not profit (quantity falls). · TR = TC is the break-even point (zero profit), not profit max. · AR = AFC has no standard economic significance for profit maximisation."],
[4,"Price Indifference Point is the sales volume at which a firm's net income is:",["Maximised","Zero (break-even)","Same under two pricing alternatives","Equal to fixed costs"],2,"Price indifference point = volume where two pricing options yield identical profit. Above it, new price is better; below it, old price is better.","Ebook","Other options: Maximised profit = profit max condition (MR=MC). · Zero profit = break-even point — different concept. · Profit equal to fixed costs = would imply zero variable profit contribution."],
[4,"First-degree price discrimination is also called:",["Group pricing","Product versioning","Perfect price discrimination","Menu pricing"],2,"1st degree = Perfect Price Discrimination: each customer charged their maximum willingness to pay — captures all consumer surplus.","Ebook","Other options: Group pricing = 3rd degree price discrimination (most common). · Product versioning and Menu pricing = 2nd degree price discrimination. · Quantity discounts = 2nd degree — not 1st degree."],
[4,"A theatre charging different ticket prices for adults, seniors, and children is an example of which degree of price discrimination?",["First-degree (perfect)","Second-degree (quantity)","Third-degree (group)","Geographical discrimination"],2,"3rd degree (Group Pricing): different prices charged to different consumer groups — theatre example is the textbook illustration. This is the most common type.","Ebook","Other options: First-degree = each individual charged their max price — theatre charges by group, not individual. · Second-degree = different prices for different quantities consumed (bulk discounts). · Geographical = different markets/regions — a type by 'nature', not a degree."],
[4,"Which of the following is NOT one of the 5 conditions required for price discrimination?",["Different elasticity in different markets","Monopoly power","Perfect information among buyers","Legal sanction"],2,"The 5 conditions: different elasticity, market imperfections, differentiated product, legal sanction, monopoly power. Perfect information among buyers would prevent discrimination (buyers would arbitrage).","Ebook","Other options: Different elasticity is required — charge more where inelastic. · Monopoly power is required — price discrimination needs market control. · Legal sanction is required in some forms (e.g., electricity board pricing)."],
[4,"In a perfectly competitive market, the individual firm's demand curve is:",["Downward sloping","Upward sloping","Perfectly elastic (horizontal)","Perfectly inelastic (vertical)"],2,"Under perfect competition, the firm is a price taker — it can sell any quantity at the market price. Demand curve is horizontal (perfectly elastic).","Ebook","Other options: Downward sloping = the market/industry demand curve, not the individual firm's curve. · Upward sloping would imply higher price causes more demand — impossible for a competitive firm. · Vertical (inelastic) would mean buyers purchase at any price — applies to necessities, not firm-level in perfect competition."],
[4,"Under monopoly, which formula shows the relationship between MR and AR?",["MR = AR + e","MR = AR [(e - 1) / e]","MR = AR × e","MR = AR / (e + 1)"],1,"MR = AR[(e-1)/e] where e = price elasticity of demand. In equilibrium, MR = MC, so MC = AR[(e-1)/e].","Ebook","Other options: MR = AR + e has no economic basis. · MR = AR × e would incorrectly scale MR above AR. · MR = AR/(e+1) is not the standard formula."],
[4,"Using the ebook example: with demand P = 1000 - 10Q and TC = 100 + 40Q + Q², the profit-maximising output is approximately:",["Q = 20","Q = 44","Q = 100","Q = 60"],1,"Setting MR = MC: (1000 - 20Q) = (40 + 2Q) → 960 = 22Q → Q ≈ 43.6 ≈ 44. Price = 1000 - 10(44) = 120. Profit = Rs.20,844.","Ebook","Other options: Q = 20 is too low — doesn't satisfy MR = MC. · Q = 100 is too high — MC would exceed MR. · Q = 60 would yield a lower profit than Q = 44."],
[4,"In monopolistic competition, economic profit in the LONG RUN tends to:",["Increase as brand differentiation strengthens","Remain constant","Approach zero due to low entry barriers","Become negative always"],2,"Low barriers attract new entrants → increases competition → profit is competed away → LR economic profit = 0. SR positive profit is temporary.","Ebook","Other options: Brand differentiation slows profit erosion but doesn't prevent it in LR. · Constant profit would require barriers — monopolistic competition has low barriers. · Negative always is too extreme — firms can earn normal (zero economic) profit in LR."],
[4,"The hallmark feature of Oligopoly pricing is:",["Perfectly elastic demand","Prices changing frequently with cost changes","Sticky (inflexible) prices that change rarely","Price equals marginal cost"],2,"Oligopoly: prices are sticky — they change less frequently than under other market structures due to mutual interdependence and collusion risks.","Ebook","Other options: Perfectly elastic demand = perfect competition — not oligopoly. · Frequent price changes = competitive markets — not oligopoly. · Price = MC = perfect competition equilibrium condition."],
[4,"Under price leadership in oligopoly, which type of leader sets price because it has the lowest costs?",["Dominant firm leader","Barometric leader","Low-cost firm leader","Collusive cartel leader"],2,"Three types of price leadership: Low-cost firm (sets low price based on cost advantage), Dominant firm (sets price due to market dominance), Barometric (signals market trends).","Ebook","Other options: Dominant firm = largest market share, not necessarily lowest cost. · Barometric = signals changes — acts as market barometer, not cost-based. · Collusive cartel = joint profit max arrangement — separate from price leadership."],
[4,"Conversion Cost Plus pricing is typically used when:",["The product requires heavy R&D","The customer provides the raw materials","Standard costs are not available","Marginal costs are zero"],1,"Conversion Cost Plus: only labour and overhead form the cost base (no materials). Used when customers supply materials — firm prices only its conversion work.","Ebook","Other options: Heavy R&D products = often use cost-plus with markup — not conversion-specific. · No standard costs available = Engineering or other estimation techniques. · Marginal costs zero = monopoly with zero MC case — unrelated to conversion pricing."],
[4,"Short-run pricing decisions (special orders) should use which cost as the floor?",["Full cost","Standard cost","Incremental (marginal) cost","Fixed cost"],2,"SR special orders: only incremental costs matter (additional materials, labour, power). Fixed costs are already committed whether or not the order is accepted.","Ebook","Other options: Full cost = LR pricing basis — not appropriate for one-time SR orders. · Standard cost = efficient operating cost — used for LR pricing. · Fixed cost is irrelevant for SR decisions — already sunk."],
[4,"Life Cycle Product Costing tracks costs from:",["First sale to last sale","Initial R&D to end of customer servicing/support","Raw material purchase to factory gate","Launch date to peak sales"],1,"Life Cycle Costing: tracks all costs from initial R&D through to when customer service/support is finally discontinued — full product lifecycle.","Ebook","Other options: First to last sale ignores pre-launch R&D costs and post-sale support. · Raw material to factory gate = manufacturing cost only — too narrow. · Launch to peak sales = only the growth phase — incomplete lifecycle."],
[4,"Under perfect competition, the market price is determined by Marshall's concept of:",["Only demand forces","Only supply forces","Both demand and supply intersecting (like two scissor blades)","Government price fixing"],2,"Marshall: 'Both elements of demand and supply are required for price determination in the same manner as both blades of scissors are required to cut cloth.'","Ebook","Other options: Only demand forces = demand-side only — Marshall explicitly rejected this. · Only supply forces = supply-side only — similarly incomplete. · Government price fixing is price control — not free market determination."],
[4,"A doctor charging different fees to rich and poor patients for the same procedure is an example of:",["Geographical price discrimination","Price discrimination by use","Personal price discrimination","Third-degree price discrimination"],2,"Personal Price Discrimination: different prices charged to different individuals for the same product/service based on their ability/willingness to pay.","Ebook","Other options: Geographical = different prices in different markets/regions. · By use = different prices for different uses of same commodity. · Third-degree = group pricing (e.g., senior/adult/child) — not individual-level."],
[4,"When a monopolist faces zero marginal cost (MC = 0), equilibrium is achieved where:",["MR is also zero and demand elasticity equals 1","Price equals zero","Output is at maximum capacity","AR equals AFC"],0,"When MC = 0, equilibrium is at MR = 0, which corresponds to the midpoint of the AR (demand) curve where price elasticity = 1.","Ebook","Other options: Price = 0 would be irrational — a monopolist always charges positive price to earn profit. · Maximum capacity = not necessarily where MR = MC = 0. · AR = AFC is not a meaningful pricing equilibrium condition."],
[4,"Geographical price discrimination includes 'dumping', which means:",["Selling defective goods at a discount","Selling the same product at a low price abroad and a higher price domestically","Selling below cost to eliminate competitors","Selling excess inventory through clearance"],1,"Dumping = geographical PD: selling in foreign markets at lower prices than in the domestic market — often to clear surplus or gain market share.","Ebook","Other options: Selling defective goods = quality-based discount — not dumping. · Below cost to eliminate competitors = predatory pricing — not necessarily dumping. · Clearance of excess inventory can involve discounts but is not the definition of dumping."],
[4,"Monopolistic competition tends to lead to heavy advertising because:",["Government mandates advertising","Products are perfectly homogeneous, requiring promotion","Products are differentiated (but similar), requiring firms to distinguish their offerings","Advertising reduces per-unit costs"],2,"Monopolistic competition: products are differentiated (but close substitutes). Firms spend heavily on advertising to convince consumers their product is superior.","Ebook","Other options: Government does not mandate advertising in this market structure. · Perfectly homogeneous products = perfect competition — no advertising needed. · Advertising can create brand loyalty but doesn't directly reduce unit costs."],
[4,"In Target Pricing, step 5 involves:",["Choosing the target price based on market","Deriving the target cost","Estimating actual cost of the product","Investigating ways to reduce actual cost to meet target cost"],3,"Step 5: If estimated actual cost > target cost → investigate cost reduction methods, redesign the product, or accept a smaller profit margin.","Ebook","Other options: Choosing target price = Step 2. · Deriving target cost = Step 3. · Estimating actual cost = Step 4."],
[5,"Accounting Profit is calculated as:",["TR - (Explicit + Implicit costs)","TR - (W + R + I + M)","Accounting Profit - Opportunity Costs","TR - Fixed Costs only"],1,"Accounting Profit = TR - (W + R + I + M): Total Revenue minus Wages, Rent, Interest, and Materials — all explicit cash payments.","Ebook","Other options: TR - (Explicit + Implicit) = Economic Profit formula. · Accounting Profit - Opportunity Cost = Pure Profit formula. · TR - Fixed Costs only ignores variable costs — incorrect."],
[5,"Pure Profit is calculated as:",["TR - Explicit costs","Accounting Profit - (Opportunity Cost + Unauthorized Payments)","TR - (Explicit + Implicit costs)","Sales Revenue - Tax"],1,"Pure Profit = Accounting Profit - (Opportunity Cost + Unauthorized Payments such as bribes). It strips out all implicit costs.","Ebook","Other options: TR - Explicit costs = Accounting Profit. · TR - (Explicit + Implicit) = Economic Profit. · Sales Revenue - Tax = Net profit after tax (accounting)."],
[5,"How does Profit differ from Returns (wages, interest) in terms of sign?",["Profit is always larger than returns","Profit can be negative; returns are always positive","Profit and returns are both always positive","Returns can be negative; profit is always positive"],1,"Profit can be negative (a loss) while returns like wages and interest are always positive by definition — one of the three ways profit differs from returns.","Ebook","Other options: Profit being always larger is incorrect — it can be negative. · Both always positive is incorrect — profit can be a loss. · Returns being negative is wrong — wages and interest are contractually guaranteed positive."],
[5,"Walker's theory of profit is also known as the:",["Innovation theory","Uncertainty-bearing theory","Rent theory (differential ability)","Dynamic theory"],2,"Walker's theory: profit is like rent — it's the reward for differential ability of entrepreneurs over the least efficient operator (analogous to land fertility).","Ebook","Other options: Innovation theory = Schumpeter. · Uncertainty-bearing theory = Knight. · Dynamic theory = Clark (profit arises in dynamic, changing economies)."],
[5,"According to Knight's Uncertainty-Bearing Theory, an entrepreneur earns profit as reward for bearing:",["Calculable (insurable) risks","Non-calculable (uninsurable) uncertainties","All types of business risks equally","Innovation-related uncertainties only"],1,"Knight distinguishes calculable risks (insured by insurance companies) from non-calculable uncertainties (e.g., demand shifts). Profit = reward for the latter.","Ebook","Other options: Calculable risks are insured — insurance companies bear them, not entrepreneurs. · All risks equally = Hawley's Risk Theory — Knight specifically excludes insurable risks. · Innovation only = Schumpeter's theory — Knight's scope is uncertainty in general."],
[5,"Hawley's Risk Theory of Profit was proposed in:",["1871","1893","1912","1934"],1,"F.B. Hawley proposed the Risk Theory of Profit in 1893: profit = reward for risk-taking; greater risk → higher expected profit.","Ebook","Other options: 1871 = no major profit theory milestone in the ebook. · 1912 = Schumpeter's Theory of Economic Development (innovation). · 1934 = not a date associated with a profit theory in this ebook."],
[5,"Schumpeter's Innovation Theory holds that profit is the reward for:",["Risk-taking in uncertain markets","Differential managerial ability","Innovation — new products, methods, markets, or materials","Bearing non-calculable uncertainty"],2,"Schumpeter: profit = innovation reward. Innovation = new product, new production technology, new markets, new raw materials, or new organisational forms.","Ebook","Other options: Risk-taking = Hawley's theory. · Differential ability = Walker's theory. · Non-calculable uncertainty = Knight's theory."],
[5,"According to Peter Drucker, which of these is NOT one of the three main purposes of profit?",["Tool for measuring performance","Source of covering costs (replacement, risk)","Aid to ensure future capital for innovation","Reward for bearing market risk"],3,"Drucker's 3 functions: (1) Measure performance, (2) Cover costs (replacement/uncertainty), (3) Secure future capital. Risk reward is Hawley's theory — not Drucker.","Ebook","Other options: Measuring performance = Drucker function 1. · Covering costs = Drucker function 2. · Securing future capital = Drucker function 3."],
[5,"Profit Maximisation requires TWO conditions to be satisfied simultaneously. They are:",["TR > TC and MR > MC","MC = MR AND MC cuts MR from below","MC = AC and TR = TC","MR > 0 and MC < 0"],1,"Two conditions: (1) MC = MR, (2) MC must cut MR from below (ensures it's a maximum, not minimum). Both must hold simultaneously.","Ebook","Other options: TR > TC ensures profit exists but doesn't identify the maximum. · MC = AC is cost minimisation, not profit maximisation. · MR > 0 and MC < 0 would be unusual — not the standard condition."],
[5,"Berle and Means (1932) argued that modern corporations are not operated to maximise profits because:",["Profits are taxed away","Managers have different goals from shareholders; shareholders have little influence","Market competition eliminates excess profits","Technology makes profit measurement impossible"],1,"Berle & Means (1932): separation of ownership (shareholders) from control (managers). Managers pursue sales growth, job security etc. — not profit maximisation.","Ebook","Other options: Taxation reduces profit but doesn't change the objective. · Market competition is a market force — not the Berle & Means argument. · Technology and measurement are not Berle & Means concerns."],
[5,"The Break-Even Point formula (in units) is:",["TR / (P - AVC)","FC / (P - AVC)","FC / TR","P / (P - AVC)"],1,"BEP (units) = Fixed Cost / (Price - AVC). The denominator (P - AVC) is the Contribution Margin per unit.","Ebook","Other options: TR / (P - AVC) would not yield units — wrong formula. · FC / TR is not a BEP formula. · P / (P - AVC) = 1 / P/V ratio — gives BEP as % of price, not units."],
[5,"In the Indian Airlines example from the ebook: fare = Rs.500, AVC = Rs.100, FC = Rs.3,00,000/month. What is the break-even number of passengers?",["500 passengers","600 passengers","750 passengers","1000 passengers"],2,"CM = 500 - 100 = Rs.400. BEP = 3,00,000 / 400 = 750 passengers per month to break even.","Ebook","Other options: 500 = FC/P (ignoring AVC) — incorrect calculation. · 600 = FC / 500 = 600 — uses price not contribution margin. · 1000 = FC / 300 — incorrect denominator."],
[5,"Total Contribution Margin (TCM) is expressed as:",["TR - TFC","TFC + TNP = TR - TVC","TR - TC","TVC - TFC"],1,"TCM = TNP + TFC = TR - TVC. Total contribution margin covers fixed costs first, then any surplus becomes net profit.","Ebook","Other options: TR - TFC would equal variable profit (not standard formula). · TR - TC = Total Net Profit (TNP), not TCM. · TVC - TFC would be negative — no meaningful interpretation."],
[5,"Which type of inflation occurs when demand exceeds production capacity?",["Cost-Push","Built-In","Demand-Pull","Structural"],2,"Demand-Pull inflation: overall demand for goods and services increases faster than the economy's production capacity — demand > supply → rising prices.","Ebook","Other options: Cost-Push = rising production input costs (wages, raw materials). · Built-In = wage-price spiral from adaptive expectations. · Structural inflation is not one of the three types in this ebook."],
[5,"Built-In (Wage-Price Spiral) inflation arises from:",["Central bank printing more money","Rising raw material costs","Adaptive expectations: workers demand higher wages as prices rise, feeding further price rises","Excess foreign investment"],2,"Built-in inflation: prices rise → workers expect more rises → demand higher wages → production costs rise → prices rise further. Self-perpetuating spiral.","Ebook","Other options: Printing money = demand-pull inflation (money supply increase). · Rising raw materials = cost-push inflation. · Foreign investment = can affect demand but not the direct cause of built-in inflation."],
[5,"The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures:",["Prices at the wholesale/producer level","Average change in selling prices received by producers","Weighted average of prices of a consumer goods basket at retail prices","Government expenditure price changes"],2,"CPI: weighted average of a basket of consumer goods (transport, food, medical care) at retail prices — measures cost of living from the buyer's perspective.","Ebook","Other options: Wholesale/producer level prices = WPI (Wholesale Price Index). · Selling prices received by producers = PPI (Producer Price Index). · Government expenditure prices = fiscal indicator — not CPI."],
[5,"Using the ebook example: if initial CPI = 54.6 (Sept 1975) and final CPI = 252.439 (Sept 2018), what was $10,000 in 1975 worth in 2018?",["$46,234","$52,000","$56,234","$30,000"],2,"Ratio = 252.439/54.6 = 4.6234. Change = 4.6234 × $10,000 = $46,234. Final value = $10,000 + $46,234 = $56,234.25.","Ebook","Other options: $46,234 = only the change in value, not the final total. · $52,000 is an incorrect calculation. · $30,000 has no mathematical basis from the given data."],
[5,"Hyperinflation is typically defined as inflation of _____ or more per month:",["10%","25%","50%","100%"],2,"Hyperinflation = inflation rate of 50% or more per month. Germany 1923 is the classic example (price level rose 1.3 billion times in one year).","Ebook","Other options: 10% monthly = high inflation but not hyperinflation. · 25% monthly = severe inflation, not the hyperinflation threshold. · 100% monthly = well above hyperinflation threshold — but 50% is the definition."],
[5,"Which group GAINS during unanticipated inflation?",["Fixed income earners (salaried employees)","Bond and debenture holders","Creditors/lenders","Debtors/borrowers"],3,"Debtors/borrowers gain: they repay loans in 'cheaper' rupees (lower real value). Creditors lose — they receive depreciated money.","Ebook","Other options: Fixed income earners LOSE — wages lag price increases. · Bond holders LOSE — fixed interest income erodes in real value. · Creditors LOSE — they receive cheaper money than they lent."],
[5,"Stocks are considered the BEST hedge against inflation because:",["Stock prices are fixed by government","Price rises pass through to company profits and stock prices","Stocks are immune to monetary policy","Dividends are adjusted for inflation automatically"],1,"Stocks: as inflation raises input/output prices, company revenues and profits rise → stock prices rise too — inflation effects are incorporated into equity prices.","Ebook","Other options: Government doesn't fix stock prices — markets do. · Stocks are affected by monetary policy (interest rate changes affect valuations). · Dividends are not automatically inflation-adjusted — they depend on company profits."],
[5,"Clark's Dynamic Theory of Profit argues that profit arises in a dynamic economy due to:",["Differential managerial ability","Risk-taking by entrepreneurs","Changes in population, capital, wants, and technology","Innovation in products and processes"],2,"Clark: dynamic economy (changing population, capital, consumer wants, production methods) creates profit opportunities. Static economy = only normal profit.","Ebook","Other options: Differential managerial ability = Walker's Rent Theory. · Risk-taking = Hawley's Risk Theory. · Innovation = Schumpeter's Innovation Theory."],
[5,"Hall and Hitch's criticism of Profit Maximisation theory found that real-world firms:",["Always maximise profits using MC=MR","Use average cost pricing (AVC + AFC + profit margin) not MC=MR","Focus only on short-run profits","Never earn profits above normal"],1,"Hall and Hitch: firms in practice use average cost principle — price = AVC + AFC + ~10% profit margin — not the MR=MC rule they've never heard of.","Ebook","Other options: Always using MC=MR contradicts Hall & Hitch's empirical finding. · Short-run focus only is a criticism but not Hall & Hitch's specific finding. · Never earning above-normal profit is incorrect — firms do earn super-normal profits."],
[5,"The Profit-Volume (P/V) ratio is defined as:",["Total Fixed Cost / Total Revenue","Contribution Margin / Price = (P - AVC) / P","Net Profit / Volume","Total Revenue / Total Variable Cost"],1,"P/V ratio = CM / Price = (P - AVC) / P. It represents the percentage of revenue available to cover fixed costs and earn profit.","Ebook","Other options: TFC / TR is not the P/V ratio. · Net Profit / Volume = profit per unit — not the standard P/V ratio. · TR / TVC has no standard significance."],
[5,"TIPS (Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities) protect against inflation by:",["Paying a higher fixed coupon in inflation","Adjusting the principal amount by the percentage of inflation","Locking in commodity prices","Converting debt to equity during inflation"],1,"TIPS: the principal amount invested is increased by the inflation rate — so real value is protected. They are low-risk government securities indexed to inflation.","Ebook","Other options: Fixed coupon doesn't protect — inflation erodes fixed payments' real value. · Locking in commodity prices = commodity futures — not TIPS. · Converting debt to equity has nothing to do with TIPS."],
[5,"The Break-Even Point as a percentage of plant capacity uses which formula?",["BEP units / (P - AVC)","BEP units / Maximum capacity","FC / TR","CM / P × 100"],1,"BEP % capacity = BEP units / Maximum capacity × 100. In the factory example: BEP = 2,500 units / 20,000 = 12.5% capacity.","Ebook","Other options: BEP / (P-AVC) would give FC/CM² — meaningless. · FC / TR = a cost-to-revenue ratio — not BEP % capacity. · CM / P × 100 = P/V ratio expressed as % — not BEP % capacity."]
  ]
};
